  "meta": {
    "title": "If infinitely many a’s, then infinitely many b’s",
    "blurb": "GF a → GF b, a Streett condition, as three priorities. The least priority seen infinitely often decides: 0 (a b recurs) accepts, 1 (a’s recur but no b) rejects, 2 (only c’s in the end) accepts. The machine only remembers the last letter.",
    "inputs": [
      {
        "w": "(ab)",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "(c)",
        "expect": "accept",
        "label": "no a at all"
      },
      {
        "w": "aaa(c)",
        "expect": "accept",
        "label": "finitely many a"
      },
      {
        "w": "(aacb)",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "(a)",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "(ac)",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "b(a)",
        "expect": "reject"
      }
    ],
    "library": {
      "author": {
        "login": "thethinkmachine"
      },
      "license": "CC-BY-4.0",
      "tags": [
        "ltl",
        "streett",
        "fairness",
        "parity"
      ],
      "difficulty": "intermediate",
      "readme": "The implication GF a → GF b is the shape of **strong fairness**: if a process is enabled infinitely often (a), it is scheduled infinitely often (b). It is naturally a *Streett* pair, and a parity condition can say it with three colours:\n\n| priority | state | what recurring it means |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 0 | last was b | b recurs — accept, whatever else happens |\n| 1 | last was a | a recurs without b — reject |\n| 2 | last was c | neither a nor b recurs — accept vacuously |\n\nLower numbers win because the least priority seen infinitely often is the one that counts. Every ω-regular language has a deterministic parity automaton, and three priorities are the fewest this one allows: it is neither deterministic Büchi nor deterministic co-Büchi, the two conditions that two priorities can express."
    }
  }
}
